---
wocument: 1
title: Coastline
lang: en-GB
theme: ./edition.woct
description: "A photo essay: full-screen pictures, a row of pictures, short text between."
---
%% RECIPE: a photo essay. Pictures lead: full-screen scenes with one picture and one line, a row
%% group that becomes a column on a phone by itself, and short text frames between. Captions do
%% more work than body copy here.
%% The pictures are stand-ins and the text is sample text: replace both with the author's.

\folio[position=top-right, progress]{The Coast Issue}

%% The cover picture, with the title on its lower rows.
\scene[name=cover, height=screen, palette=night, folio=hide]{
  \figure[src=./pictures/landscape-dusk.jpg, alt="The coast at dusk", layer=background, focus="50% 65%", loading=eager]
  \frame[cols=1-8, rows=5-6, cols@phone=all, valign=bottom, enter=rise]{
    \kicker{Photo essay}
    \headline{Coastline}
    \deck{Six pictures from a winter on the edge of the land.}
  }
}

%% A short introduction on the body's columns, then a row of pictures: side by side on wide
%% screens, one under another on a phone. One scene, so one running head (every scene draws its own).
%% valign=stretch gives the row one height: pictures on the grid's outer edges reach into the margin.
\scene[name=intro]{
  \frame[cols=4-9, cols@tablet=2-7, cols@phone=all]{
    \lede{The coast changes more in a winter than in the rest of the year together. These pictures were made over four months, at the same few places, in whatever light the weather allowed.}
  }
  \group[layout=row, cols=all, valign=stretch]{
    \figure[src=./pictures/portrait-valley.jpg, alt="A valley running down to the sea under low cloud", min-width=4col, ratio=4:5]{
      \caption{The valley in January, under low cloud.}
    }
    \figure[src=./pictures/square-portrait.jpg, alt="The ridge above the station, grey on grey", min-width=4col, ratio=4:5]{
      \caption{The ridge above the station.}
    }
    \figure[src=./pictures/landscape-valley.jpg, alt="Fog filling the valley floor", min-width=4col, ratio=4:5, focus="40% 60%"]{
      \caption{Fog in the morning, gone by ten.}
    }
  }
}

%% One picture, one line: an interlude from the theme's parent.
\scene[name=night, parent=interlude]{
  \figure[src=./pictures/landscape-night.jpg, alt="Stars over the hills on a clear night", layer=background, focus="50% 60%"]
  \frame[cols=2-9, rows=4-5, cols@tablet=1-7, cols@phone=all, valign=center]{
    \pullquote{On the clear nights you could see every light along the coast.}
  }
}

%% A picture with its text beside it, then the close.
\scene[name=tower, folio=hide]{
  \figure[src=./pictures/cutout-tower.png, alt="The tower, cut out against the page", cols=1-5, cols@tablet=2-7, cols@phone=all, top=0, max-width=480px]{
    \caption{The tower, the one thing in every picture that did not change.}
  }
  \frame[cols=7-12, cols@tablet=all, cols@phone=all, top=4bl, top@tablet=auto, top@phone=auto]{
    \subhead{Notes}
    Every picture was made within a mile of the station, between November and February. The light was rarely good for more than an hour a day, and some weeks it was never good at all.

    The tower appears in most of them by accident: it is the tallest thing for miles, and the eye goes to it wherever the camera points.
    \rule
    \credit{Pictures and text: A. Photographer}
  }
}
